<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Lancaster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1399">1399</a>–<a href="/years/1471">1471</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Lancaster was a branch of the English royal house of Plantagenet, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster</a>. It held the throne from 1399, when Henry IV deposed Richard II, through Henry V, victor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a>, to Henry VI. Its rivalry with the House of York led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, and its line ended with Henry VI's death in 1471.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-lancaster.jpg" alt="House of Lancaster" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_Edmund_Crouchback,_Earl_of_Leicester_and_Lancaster.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first house of Lancaster was created in 1267, when King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> gave the Earldom of Lancaster to his second son Edmund Crouchback, who already held the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-de-montfort-6th-earl-of-leicester">Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester</a>, forfeited after the Second Barons' War. Edmund's son Thomas, 2nd Earl of Lancaster, added the Earldom of Lincoln through his wife and became the most powerful nobleman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with an income of £11,000 a year, double that of the next wealthiest earl. His conflict with his cousin Edward II ended in his execution.</p><p>The second house descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>, who married Blanche of Lancaster, heiress of the first house. Edward III had married his sons to wealthy English heiresses, and this match gave John the dukedom and the Lancastrian wealth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>John's son Henry usurped the throne in 1399 as Henry IV. Historians debate the accession, some seeing it as a cause of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a> because force had broken the principles the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">Plantagenets</a> had upheld for two and a half centuries. His advisers, led by William Thirning, dissuaded him from claiming the throne by conquest. They argued instead that he was heir male to Henry III through Edmund Crouchback, said to have been the elder son set aside for a deformity; the committee he set up found no evidence for this.</p><p>Henry V renewed the claim to the French crown. He captured Harfleur, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a> although outnumbered, recaptured much of Normandy and secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a> and a marriage to Catherine of Valois. He died of dysentery at Vincennes in 1422. During the minority of Henry VI the Lancastrians split over policy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> died in prison in 1447. After the French victories at Formigny in 1450 and Castillon in 1453, the house lost all its French holdings except Calais and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The male line ended when Edward of Westminster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, was killed in battle against supporters of the House of York in 1471 and Henry VI died or was murdered in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Descent through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt">John of Gaunt</a>'s daughter Philippa continued in the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal, while the Lancastrian cause passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry Tudor</a>, a descendant of the Beauforts, who established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">House of Tudor</a>.</p><p>The Lancastrians founded Eton College and King's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. Historians regret that Shakespeare's partly fictionalised history plays shape modern views of the dynasty more than scholarship does. The term Lancastrian was not used at the time, and doubts remain whether the red rose was really their emblem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Lancaster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
